<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PER-TAKE VOLUME AND FADE ARE AUDIBLE ON A COMPED COLUMN (QBX-185).

  THE DEFECT THIS GATES. A take's volume, pan, fade and gain envelope are
  properties of the take's own WINDOW: `set-clip-volume take=N` writes them and
  the model reads them back. They reached the mix through
  `STrack::refreshClipGainCurves`, which composes the gain for a CLIP ENTRY —
  one entry per placement — and read it from `SClipWindow::parametersOf`, i.e.
  from the ACTIVE take. `twCompColumn` renders each region from whichever take
  the map selects, but the gain had already been decided one level above it,
  per clip and not per region. So on a comped column:

    * an inactive take's own volume, pan and fade were carried, serialized and
      editable, and never heard;
    * comping two takes recorded at different levels gave ONE level to the
      whole clip;
    * the clip's fade belonged to whichever take happened to be active.

  Pre-existing since proposal 43 N2 made a map audible, and the COMMON case
  since mode A (P2, QBX-180), where every recording over existing material
  produces a comped column.

  THE SHAPE. One 4 s column over test_sawtooth.wav, both takes FULL extent and
  both reading the source from 0, so each take's own domain IS the column's and
  a region plays the source second it sits over. Per-source-second RMS of the
  fixture: 0.0667 / 0.1764 / 0.2906 / 0.4055.

  Map `96000 -> 1`, active take 0:
    column [0, 2 s) -> take 0 -> 0.0667, 0.1764
    column [2 s, 4 s) -> take 1 -> 0.2906, 0.4055

  WHY BOTH TAKES READ THE SAME SOURCE AT THE SAME POSITION. It makes the two
  takes acoustically INDISTINGUISHABLE, which is the point: every assertion
  below is then about the GAIN and nothing else. A fixture whose takes differed
  in level would let a wrong take sounding pass for a right take mis-gained.

  WHICH CLAIMS BITE THE UNMODIFIED BUILD, measured rather than assumed. Claim 1
  (two windows) and claim 3's long-fade pair are the ones that FAIL on `main`.
  Claims 2, 3's short fade and 4 all PASS there, and they are kept anyway
  because they gate the OTHER half of the fix — that it is applied per region
  and only once. Claim 2 in particular passes on `main` by coincidence: with
  both takes at the same trim, "the active take's trim over the whole clip" and
  "each take's trim over its own regions" agree, so it is a double-application
  guard rather than a defect detector. Said out loud here because a case whose
  every assertion passes before the fix is a case that gates nothing, and
  knowing WHICH ones do the work is the difference.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="take_gain_per_region">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- take 1: FULL extent, source from 0, and NOT active. -->
    <add-take clip="0,0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" startOffset="0"
              index="-1" activate="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="direct" takes="2" activeTake="0"
                        extents="0-192000;0-192000" duration="192000"/>

    <set-comp-map clip="0,0">
      <seg at="96000" take="1"/>
    </set-comp-map>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="96000:1" activeTake="0"
                        takeAtPos="0" expectTake="0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" takeAtPos="96000" expectTake="1"/>

    <!-- THE BASELINE, at 0 dB everywhere. Four source seconds, in order. -->
    <render filename="tgpr_flat.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.26" maxRms="0.33"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.36" maxRms="0.45"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 1: THE INACTIVE TAKE'S OWN VOLUME IS HEARD, over its own
         regions and NOWHERE ELSE. -20 dB is a factor of 0.1, so take 1's two
         seconds drop to 0.0291 / 0.0406 while take 0's stay put.

         ON `main` ALL FOUR WINDOWS ARE UNCHANGED: the entry's gain came from
         the ACTIVE take (take 0), so an edit to take 1 reached the model and
         not the mix. The third and fourth windows are the failing lines. -->
    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="1" volumeDb="-20"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_t1_quiet.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- take 0's half is UNTOUCHED, which is the other half of "per region":
         a fix that applied take 1's trim to the whole clip would fail here. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.024" maxRms="0.034"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.034" maxRms="0.047"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 2: THE ACTIVE TAKE'S VOLUME IS ALSO PER REGION, and this is the
         claim that catches a double application. Take 0 at -20 dB too, so now
         EVERY window is a tenth of the baseline. If the clip entry still
         carried the active take's trim AND the column applied it, take 0's
         half would be a HUNDREDTH (0.00667 / 0.01764) and the first two
         windows would fail low. -->
    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="0" volumeDb="-20"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_both_quiet.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_both_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0055" maxRms="0.0080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_both_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.015" maxRms="0.021"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_both_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.024" maxRms="0.034"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_both_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.034" maxRms="0.047"/>

    <!-- ...and back to 0 dB restores the baseline EXACTLY. A per-region gain
         that left anything behind would show here, and byte-identity is the
         strongest form of "nothing else moved". -->
    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="0" volumeDb="0"/>
    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="1" volumeDb="0"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_restored.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="tgpr_restored.wav" expected="tgpr_flat.wav"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 3: `set-clip-fade take=` IS AUDIBLE on the inactive take, and in
         THE TAKE'S OWN domain. Take 1's fade-in is 96000 frames (2 s) over its
         own 4 s window, so it ramps from the take's frame 0 — which is the
         COLUMN's frame 0, where take 1 is not even sounding. By the time take
         1's region begins at 96000 the ramp is at its midpoint, and it reaches
         full gain at the take's own 96000... i.e. exactly where the region
         starts. So the region's two seconds are shaped by the TAIL of the
         fade, not by a fade restarted at the region's edge.

         This is the assertion that says WHOSE domain the fade is read in, and
         it is the one a plausible wrong implementation gets wrong: a fade
         applied per REGION would start the ramp at 96000 and make the third
         window much quieter than the fourth by a different ratio. -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="96000" fadeOut="0"
                    shape="linear"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_t1_fade.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- take 0's half untouched again. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <!-- The fade is DONE by the time take 1 sounds (it completes at the take's
         own frame 96000), so take 1's regions are at full gain and these two
         windows match the baseline. That is what "the take's own domain" buys:
         a region-relative fade would be ramping here instead. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.26" maxRms="0.33"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.36" maxRms="0.45"/>

    <!-- ...and a fade that DOES overlap the region is audible. Take 1's
         LINEAR fade-in over its whole 4 s window is still ramping through its
         own second 2 s, which is where its regions are: the ramp runs 0.5 to
         0.75 across the third window and 0.75 to 1.0 across the fourth, so
         both are scaled DOWN while take 0's half stays put.

         MEASURED, not predicted: 0.187509 and 0.360758, against the baseline's
         0.290568 and 0.405489. The bounds below exclude the baseline values
         deliberately — a range wide enough to hold both would gate nothing,
         which is what the first draft of these two lines did. -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="192000" fadeOut="0"
                    shape="linear"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_t1_fade_long.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade_long.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.055" maxRms="0.080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade_long.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade_long.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.175" maxRms="0.205"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_fade_long.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.345" maxRms="0.378"/>

    <!-- Clear it, and the baseline comes back byte for byte. -->
    <set-clip-fade clip="0,0" take="1" fadeIn="0" fadeOut="0"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_restored2.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="tgpr_restored2.wav"
                           expected="tgpr_flat.wav"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 5: THE TAKE'S OWN PAN IS HEARD, PER REGION AND PER CHANNEL.
         The pan law (proposal 49 D1) is centre-unity BALANCE: at p = -1 the
         left gain is 1 and the right is EXACTLY 0.0, not cos(pi/2). So take
         1's regions keep channel 0 untouched and lose channel 1 entirely,
         while take 0's half keeps both — which no per-clip pan can express,
         because a clip has one pan.

         This is also the only assertion here that is PER CHANNEL, and it is
         what says the factor is indexed by the OUTPUT channel rather than by
         the take's source channel: test_sawtooth's two channels are identical,
         so a build indexing by the source channel would give both outputs the
         LEFT gain and channel 1 would read 0.290568 instead of silence. -->
    <set-clip-pan clip="0,0" take="1" pan="-1"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_t1_left.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- take 0's half: both channels untouched. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_left.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_left.wav" channel="1"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>
    <!-- take 1's half: channel 0 full, channel 1 silent. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_left.wav" channel="0"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.36" maxRms="0.45"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_t1_left.wav" channel="1"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0005"/>

    <!-- ...and centre restores the baseline byte for byte. -->
    <set-clip-pan clip="0,0" take="1" pan="0"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_restored3.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="tgpr_restored3.wav"
                           expected="tgpr_flat.wav"/>

    <!-- CLAIM 4: THE DEGENERATE CASE DOES NOT MOVE. With the map cleared and
         every take full, `getRootComponent` hands out the ACTIVE take's own
         component and never builds a column at all (the resolution rule, §4.1)
         — so the active take's trim must still reach the mix through the CLIP
         ENTRY exactly as it always has. This is the half of the fix that is
         about what did NOT change, and the AC that forbids re-freezing a
         golden. -->
    <set-comp-map clip="0,0"/>
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" comp="" activeTake="0"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_degen_flat.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_degen_flat.wav"
                         startFrame="96000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.26" maxRms="0.33"/>

    <set-clip-volume clip="0,0" take="0" volumeDb="-20"/>
    <render filename="tgpr_degen_quiet.wav" format="wav" quality="10"
            durationSec="4.5"/>
    <!-- The ACTIVE take's trim over the WHOLE clip, which is what an
         un-comped column means. A tenth in every window. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_degen_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.0055" maxRms="0.0080"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="tgpr_degen_quiet.wav"
                         startFrame="144000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.034" maxRms="0.047"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
